<mjml lang="en">
  <mj-head>
    <!--
      PROBE — two open questions, one send (2026-08-22).

      ══════════════════ VERDICTS — ALL CLAIMS MEASURED ══════════════════
      Sent 2026-08-24 as EN templateId 631; EoA test XXhlSbq1e18ponDRl1UQMK…
      (17 clients). Read from the raw renders and the delivered HTML:

      V — §4 IS RIGHT, and it is not magnitude-dependent. Outlook 2021 (Word)
          ignored the horizontal padding at BOTH magnitudes: V2 rendered
          0..471 and V3 0..535 against V1's correct 64..535 (positions
          relative to the email's left edge, measured from the PNG pixels).
          Every other client honored it (Apple Mail / Gmail.com / Outlook.com:
          V2 64..535, V3 32..567). Consequence: the four catalog blocks then
          shipping the padding shape — both background-image CTA Heroes and
          both Photo Banners w/ CTA — were flush in Outlook desktop, and were
          converted to rails the same day (TPL f81a7b9).
      T1 — EN INLINES a top-level rule from the page-template head, exactly as
          it does the CSS Editor field: the delivered HTML carries
          style="…display: none…" on the .mobile-only div. The fork's plain
          copy therefore covers the style-stripping audience, and the
          data-en-tools-template-css marker survived delivery.
      T2 — the fork FORKS on all 17 clients with no styles.css in the email:
          desktop bar only on desktop clients, mobile bar only on iPhone and
          Android Gmail, never both, never neither.
      ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

      Not a catalog page. Build, then send dist/probe_vrect-and-template-head_live.html
      through EN to Email on Acid. Read outlook2021_win11_* FIRST for the V series
      (that is the whole point of it), then applemail16_* / gmailcom-* for the T series,
      then the delivered HTML at /app/acidtest/display/email_html/<TEST_ID> for T1.

      ─── V SERIES — can Outlook honor horizontal padding inside a v:rect? ───

      MJML compiles a background-url section into <v:rect><v:textbox inset="0,0,0,0">.
      MJML-AUTHORING-GUIDE.md §4 says, as measured fact: "Outlook cannot honor
      horizontal section padding inside a v:rect. Give background sections
      vertical-only padding and fake the gutters with an mj-group of narrow spacer
      columns around the content column."

      The catalog does not agree with itself. Countdown Block and Video Block follow
      the rule (spacer rails, no horizontal section padding). FOUR blocks do not, and
      have not for some time: both background-image CTA Heroes (16px 32px 16px and
      264px 32px 16px) and both Photo Banners w/ CTA (48px 32px 48px, 350px 32px 48px).
      Progress Meter Block briefly joined them at 32px 64px on 2026-08-22 and was
      reverted to rails the same day, which is what surfaced the contradiction.

      So either the guide is stale and four blocks are fine, or four blocks carry an
      Outlook defect nobody has looked for. Guessing costs geometry either way, so
      this measures it.

      HOW TO READ IT. Every variant puts a SKY (#5DD8D8) box at the start of its copy.
      The box's LEFT EDGE IS the content edge — it is the instrument. Against the photo
      you can see exactly where the content starts without measuring anything.

        V1 CONTROL     rails, vertical-only padding — the shape §4 prescribes.
                       Sky box starts 64px in. This is what "correct" looks like.
        V2 UNDER TEST  no rails, padding="32px 64px" — the shape §4 forbids, at the
                       magnitude Progress Meter briefly shipped.
        V3 SHIPPED     no rails, padding="16px 32px 16px" — the exact shape and
                       magnitude the four live blocks carry today.

      VERDICT RULE. In Outlook 2021/Win:
        V2 and V3 boxes start where V1's does  → §4 is STALE. Correct the guide, leave
                                                 the four blocks alone, and Progress
                                                 Meter may go back to padding.
        V2 and V3 boxes start at the photo edge → §4 is RIGHT. The four blocks have a
                                                 real defect and need rails.
        V3 correct but V2 flush                → it is magnitude-dependent, which no
                                                 one has claimed. Record the threshold.
      Check every other client too: if V2/V3 look right everywhere EXCEPT Outlook, that
      is the guide's claim reproduced exactly.

      ─── T SERIES — does the page-template head carry the viewport fork? ───

      styles.css ships as the Template Styles BLOCK, in the body. An email an editor
      assembles WITHOUT that block has none of it, and a viewport fork fails open: with
      neither half of the hide/reveal pair applied, BOTH copies render and the reader
      sees the content twice. On 2026-08-22 the fork moved into the page-template head
      behind data-en-tools-template-css, which the importer exempts from extraction.

      This probe carries NO styles.css. The <style> below is the only fork CSS in it,
      so the T series tests the fork exactly as an editor would meet it in an email
      built without the styles block.

        T1  Does EN INLINE a top-level rule from the TEMPLATE head, the way it inlines
            the CSS Editor field? This is the one thing recorded as UNVERIFIED when the
            fork moved. Read the DELIVERED HTML, not a screenshot: find the element
            carrying class="mobile-only" and check whether EN put style="display:none"
            on it. If it did NOT, the plain copy is doing nothing and the fork rests on
            the wrapped 9998px copy alone — which still covers every client that keeps
            head styles, but loses the style-stripping audience (Gmail app on
            non-Google accounts, most clients after a forward).

        T2  Does the fork actually fork? Exactly ONE of the two labelled bars should be
            visible per client. Two bars = the pair failed open. Zero bars = the hide
            won everywhere and the reveal never fired.

      Colours are brand palette only (CLAUDE.md → Instrument colors): Sky #5DD8D8 marks
      the content edge, Sun #F7931E labels a variant under test, Fern #39B54A a control,
      Earth #362229 for label text.
    -->
    <mj-breakpoint width="600px" />
    <mj-title>PROBE — v:rect padding + template-head fork</mj-title>
    <mj-attributes>
      <mj-text color="#362229" font-size="16px" font-family="Tahoma, sans-serif" line-height="22px" css-class="wysiwyg" />
      <mj-image padding="0" />
    </mj-attributes>

    <!-- The fork under test. data-en-tools-template-css keeps it in the template
         head instead of being extracted into the Template Styles block. Order is
         load-bearing: plain hide, wrapped hide, then the reveal. -->
    <mj-raw>
      <style type="text/css" data-en-tools-template-css>
        .mobile-only { display: none; }
        @media only screen and (max-width: 9998px) {
          .mobile-only { display: none !important; }
        }
        @media (max-width: 599px) {
          .desktop-only { display: none !important; }
          .mobile-only { display: block !important; }
        }
      </style>
    </mj-raw>
  </mj-head>

  <mj-body background-color="#ffffff">

    <mj-section background-color="#362229" padding="12px 16px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text color="#8CC63F" align="center" padding="0">
          <p style="font-size:14px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:3px;font-family:Menlo,Consolas,monospace;margin:0;">V SERIES — v:rect HORIZONTAL PADDING</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- V1 CONTROL — rails, vertical-only padding. The shape guide §4 prescribes. -->
    <mj-section background-color="#39B54A" padding="8px 16px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text color="#ffffff" padding="0">
          <p style="font-weight:700;margin:0;">V1 CONTROL — spacer rails, padding="32px 0 32px". Sky box should start 64px in.</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>
    <mj-section background-url="assets/photo-forest.jpg" background-size="cover" background-repeat="no-repeat" background-position="center center" padding="32px 0 32px">
      <mj-group>
        <mj-column width="64px">&nbsp;</mj-column>
        <mj-column width="472px">
          <mj-text container-background-color="#5DD8D8" padding="8px">
            <p style="margin:0;font-weight:700;">V1 — this box's LEFT EDGE is the content edge.</p>
          </mj-text>
        </mj-column>
        <mj-column width="64px">&nbsp;</mj-column>
      </mj-group>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- V2 UNDER TEST — no rails, horizontal section padding at 64px. -->
    <mj-section background-color="#F7931E" padding="8px 16px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text color="#362229" padding="0">
          <p style="font-weight:700;margin:0;">V2 UNDER TEST — no rails, padding="32px 64px". Flush against the photo edge in Outlook = §4 is right.</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>
    <mj-section background-url="assets/photo-forest.jpg" background-size="cover" background-repeat="no-repeat" background-position="center center" padding="32px 64px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text container-background-color="#5DD8D8" padding="8px">
          <p style="margin:0;font-weight:700;">V2 — compare this box's left edge to V1's.</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- V3 SHIPPED — the exact shape the four live blocks carry today. -->
    <mj-section background-color="#F7931E" padding="8px 16px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text color="#362229" padding="0">
          <p style="font-weight:700;margin:0;">V3 SHIPPED SHAPE — no rails, padding="16px 32px 16px", as the two CTA Heroes and two Photo Banners ship today. Box should start 32px in.</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>
    <mj-section background-url="assets/photo-forest.jpg" background-size="cover" background-repeat="no-repeat" background-position="center center" padding="16px 32px 16px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text container-background-color="#5DD8D8" padding="8px">
          <p style="margin:0;font-weight:700;">V3 — 32px in, or flush?</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <mj-section background-color="#362229" padding="12px 16px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text color="#8CC63F" align="center" padding="0">
          <p style="font-size:14px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:3px;font-family:Menlo,Consolas,monospace;margin:0;">T SERIES — TEMPLATE-HEAD VIEWPORT FORK</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <mj-section background-color="#F5FAF1" padding="8px 16px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text padding="0">
          <p style="margin:0;">This page carries NO styles.css. The fork below is served only by the
          page-template head. Exactly ONE bar should be visible. Two bars = the pair failed open;
          zero bars = the reveal never fired.</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- T2 — the fork itself. Word ignores @media AND display:none, so the
         mobile-only half must sit inside the revealed conditional or Outlook
         shows both (guide §2a). -->
    <mj-section css-class="desktop-only" background-color="#006837" padding="16px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text color="#ffffff" align="center" padding="0">
          <p style="font-weight:700;margin:0;">T2 — DESKTOP BAR. Correct on desktop clients. If you also see the mobile bar, the fork failed open.</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>
    <mj-raw><!--[if !mso]><!--></mj-raw>
    <mj-section css-class="mobile-only" background-color="#5DD8D8" padding="16px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text color="#362229" align="center" padding="0">
          <p style="font-weight:700;margin:0;">T2 — MOBILE BAR. Correct under 600px. Seeing this on desktop means the hide did not apply.</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>
    <mj-raw><!--<![endif]--></mj-raw>

    <mj-section background-color="#F5FAF1" padding="8px 16px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text padding="0">
          <p style="margin:0;">T1 is read from the DELIVERED HTML, not from this render: find the element
          with class="mobile-only" and check whether EN added style="display:none" to it. Inlined = EN
          treats template-head CSS like the CSS Editor field, and the style-stripping audience is covered.
          Not inlined = the fork rests on the wrapped 9998px copy alone.</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

  </mj-body>
</mjml>
